EXT. CANYON CROSSING - HIGH NOON

Red rock walls. A wooden bridge over a dry creek bed, planks bleached silver, rope rails frayed. Heat shimmers. Crows wheel overhead.

Six people on horseback and on foot gather at the near end of the bridge.

A woman in a duster, tall, hat low, hands resting on the saddle horn. This is the STRANGER (30s, dust on her lashes, two revolvers). Beside her, a boy leads a mule: WENDELL (teen, freckled, tin cup tied to his belt). At the far end of the bridge, three men block the way: SHERIFF HOLLIS (50s, mustache, shotgun), DEPUTY CRANE (20s, nervous, gun too big) and a stout man in a vest, MAYOR PETTIBONE (60s, watch chain, sweating).

Wind. A creaking plank. Nobody moves.

HOLLIS
Toll's two dollars.

STRANGER
Since when?

HOLLIS
Since this morning.

CRANE
It's for the bridge fund.

STRANGER
The bridge looks fine to me.

PETTIBONE
It's an investment.

The Stranger swings off her horse. Her boots thud on the plank. She walks a few steps, stops, and looks down at the creek bed forty feet below.

STRANGER
It's a long way to fall.

HOLLIS
Nobody's falling.

STRANGER
Then why the toll?

HOLLIS
Because this is my bridge.

WENDELL
Ma'am, we could go around.

STRANGER
There is no around, Wendell.

She says his name softly. Hollis notices, glances at the boy, looks back at her, and for the first time his mustache twitches.

HOLLIS
You know the boy?

STRANGER
I do.

HOLLIS
Who's he to you?

STRANGER
Nephew.

Crane's grip tightens on his revolver. Pettibone steps back. The Stranger takes two more steps and now she is in the middle of the bridge, a long shadow stretched behind her.

STRANGER (CONT'D)
My name's Ada Morrow. My brother built this bridge.

HOLLIS
(slowly)
Morrow built it?

ADA
He did. With his hands. Before you hung him.

Silence. A crow lands on the rope rail. Hollis's finger finds the trigger.

ADA (CONT'D)
So I'll cross. And you'll stand aside. Or the bridge comes down with all of us.

She pulls a stick of dynamite from her duster. Cold, calm, a match already between her fingers. Crane gasps. Pettibone sits down on the planks. Wendell lets go of the mule.

On the canyon rim above, a lone figure watches: WIDOW LANGSTON (70s, black shawl, rifle across her knees). Her voice carries down the rock.

WIDOW LANGSTON (O.S.)
Let her cross, Hollis. Or I will.

HOLLIS
You wouldn't.

ADA
It's my brother's bridge.
